  "title": "Odd one out",
  "format": "build",
  "skills": [
    "logic.parity"
  ],
  "brief": {
    "story": "The serial link adds a parity bit so the receiver can spot a flipped bit: the parity LED is lit when an odd number of the three data switches are on. XOR chained through three inputs does exactly that, but the chain is broken.",
    "goal": "Wire gate 1’s output (pin 3) into gate 2 (pin 4) so LED1 lights for an odd number of switches on.",
    "datasheet": {
      "title": "Odd parity of 3 bits",
      "rows": [
        [
          "Gate 1",
          "SA XOR SB → pin 3 (column 12)"
        ],
        [
          "Gate 2",
          "pins 4 (column 13) and 5 (SC) → 6"
        ],
        [
          "Lit for",
          "1 or 3 switches on"
        ]
      ]
    }
  },
  "hints": [
    "XOR is 1 when its inputs differ, which is the same as “an odd number of them are 1”. Chaining XORs counts odd for any number of inputs.",
    "Gate 1 does SA XOR SB on pin 3, column 12. Gate 2 needs that on pin 4, column 13.",
    "One wire from column 12 to column 13."
  ],
