  "debrief": "Chained XORs compute parity: 1 when an odd number of inputs are 1. A sender adds that bit; if one bit flips on the way, the receiver’s parity no longer matches and it knows. Serial ports, memory chips (ECC) and RAID disk arrays all use parity, from three bits to thousands."
